title: Kinds of thing
description: Instruction, rule, skill, memory, knowledge files and settings. What each is for, where each can sit, and how they differ.
sections:
  - title: Instruction
    description: Standing guidance on how to behave. A CLAUDE.md in Claude Code, an instructions field in the chat app.
    body: |
      Always in context for its reach, whether or not it is relevant to the task, so it should be short and universal. Claude treats it as context, not enforced configuration.
    children:
      - title: Where it can sit in Claude Code
        body: |
          | Place | Owner | Loads |
          |---|---|---|
          | `~\.claude\CLAUDE.md` | Me, everywhere | At start |
          | An ancestor folder's CLAUDE.md | Team, everything below it | At start |
          | The repo's CLAUDE.md | Team, this repo | At start |
          | `.claude\CLAUDE.md` | Team, this repo | At start, beside the root one |
          | `CLAUDE.local.md` | Me, this repo | At start |
          | A subfolder's CLAUDE.md | Team, that subfolder | When Claude works there |
          | A file named by an `@import` line | Same as the importer | Inlined into the importer |
      - title: Where it can sit in the chat app
        body: |
          Settings > General for every chat. A Project's instructions field for chats anchored to that Project. A folder-anchored chat may read the folder's CLAUDE.md and may still have the old per-folder instructions field. Both are pending verification.
  - title: Rule
    description: An instruction in its own file, optionally scoped to paths. Claude Code only.
    body: |
      Every `.md` file in `~\.claude\rules` or the repo's `.claude\rules` loads like an extra CLAUDE.md. A `paths:` list in its frontmatter makes it wait until Claude touches a matching file. Rule is barely a kind of its own: it differs from an instruction only in file layout and in the optional path filter. Use one to keep topic files separate, or to keep guidance out of the way until it applies.
  - title: Skill
    description: A procedure for one kind of task, with its scripts, templates and references.
    body: |
      Only the frontmatter description loads at start, so Claude knows when to reach for it. The body and the files beside it load when the skill is invoked, by typing its name or by Claude choosing it. A skill costs almost nothing until used, which is why a multi-step procedure needed now and then belongs here rather than in a CLAUDE.md.
    children:
      - title: Sources
        body: |
          | Source | Owner |
          |---|---|
          | `~\.claude\skills` | Me, everywhere |
          | `.claude\skills` | Team, this repo |
          | A plugin | Whoever installed it, at that scope |
          | `~\.claude\skills\synced` | Skills uploaded on claude.ai, synced down for Claude Code |
          | Settings > Skills | Me in the chat app, or provisioned by the org |
  - title: Memory
    description: What Claude wrote down from earlier sessions.
    body: |
      Claude Code's auto-memory is a folder of markdown files under `~\.claude\projects\<key>\memory`, one file per fact with a `type` of user, feedback, project or reference, and a MEMORY.md index. It is machine-local and per repository, shared across worktrees, never shared with teammates or cloud sessions.
    children:
      - title: Three things called memory
        body: |
          | Name | What it is | Holds facts? |
          |---|---|---|
          | Auto-memory, `~\.claude\projects\<key>\memory` | Claude Code's built-in notes, machine-local | Yes |
          | A memory brief, such as `claude\memory.brief` in orchestration | A curated memory for one repo, a Studio brief | Yes |
          | `.memory` files, such as `Neogrids.memory` | A Studio kind that is a working-memory lens: saved locks and a journal over a folder | No |
      - title: Regimes
        body: |
          - **Auto-memory on.** The default. Claude decides what to save; the index loads every session, topic files on demand.
          - **Auto-memory relocated.** `autoMemoryDirectory` in a settings file moves the folder, for example into the repo, git-ignored, where the Studio shows it.
          - **Auto-memory off, a memory brief.** `autoMemoryEnabled: false`, a brief in the repo, and a hook that injects its outline at SessionStart and its area names on every prompt. The orchestration regime.
          - **Account memory.** Settings > Memory in the chat app, with a space per Project.
          - **The app's per-folder memory.** Kept in the desktop app's database for what was a Cowork project. Whether it survived the merge is pending.
      - title: The write gate
        body: |
          - **Claude writes on its own.** The default for auto-memory.
          - **Shown first, written only on a yes.** A rule such as "ask before saving a memory", or orchestration's playbook event "Something should be committed to memory". Followed, not enforced.
          - **A hook or permission rule refuses the write.** A PreToolUse hook on writes under the memory folder, or the path in the `ask` or `deny` permission list. The only hard guarantee.
  - title: Knowledge files
    description: Reference material to consult, not guidance.
    body: |
      The documents uploaded into a claude.ai Project: PDFs, spreadsheets, text, code. They say nothing about behaviour; they are the source of truth for a topic. Small totals load whole at chat start; large totals are searched and only the matching passages come in. The nearest Claude Code equivalent is the folder itself, the files Claude reads and greps as it works, which is why a folder anchor and a Project anchor are partly substitutes.
  - title: Settings
    description: Permissions, hooks, environment, feature toggles. The one kind Claude never reads.
    body: |
      Settings are read by the harness and enforced. They decide what Claude may do, not what it knows. A hook is the one setting that puts text into context: whatever a SessionStart or UserPromptSubmit hook prints is added to the conversation.
    children:
      - title: Tiers, highest precedence first
        body: |
          | Tier | File | Who |
          |---|---|---|
          | Managed | managed-settings.json, MDM, or the claude.ai console | The organisation |
          | Command line | `claude --settings` | Me, this session |
          | Project local | `.claude\settings.local.json` | Me, this repo |
          | Shared project | `.claude\settings.json` | Everyone in the repo |
          | User | `~\.claude\settings.json` | Me, every project |
  - title: Choosing between them
    body: |
      - Every session must know it: a CLAUDE.md.
      - Only matters in one part of the repo, or you want topic files: a rule, path-scoped if it should stay out of the way otherwise.
      - A multi-step procedure needed now and then, especially with scripts: a skill.
      - Something Claude learned that the team should follow: move it from memory to a CLAUDE.md or a rule.
      - Something that must be impossible rather than discouraged: a hook or a permission rule, never prose.
